The Gherkin representation is excellent for capturing the high-level behavior and acceptance criteria of the system, which is beneficial for stakeholders to understand and for writing tests that reflect the requirements. It is also relatively simple and straightforward, making it easier to write and maintain.

On the other hand, xUnit Test Cases are highly suitable for writing executable tests that directly validate the functionality of the system components, including the workflow routing, machine learning predictions, and database operations. They are essential for ensuring that the system meets its specifications and can handle the expected volume of consultation requests. Given the need for both high-level behavioral understanding and low-level code-level validation, these two representations together provide a comprehensive approach to testing the requirements.
